-- Loyalty: a climbing club with referrals, monthly dues, and gear purchases —
-- the fifth Velle spec, written to put the prover's vocabulary on display in
-- business terms. Every construct here that LOOKS like it could race or
-- recurse forever is accepted because a stated fact about the data proves it
-- safe: a `never` the compiler spends as a proof input (README §21), a
-- predecessor recurrence whose ordering lives in a datum rather than in
-- firing order (README §19), sibling rules whose effects provably push one
-- way, and a condition-only read the commit semantics pin (evaluation.md,
-- "Processing one commit").
--
-- Comment convention (as in payments.velle): plain comments tell the business
-- story; `velle:` comments note the language machinery on show.

-- ── Members and referrals ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Members join by referral: the join is the input, the member record is what
-- the club keeps (its `tier` is system-maintained, so it lives on the
-- unexposed record — V21). Nobody recruits themselves — the club says so
-- once, as a fact about the data, and the compiler spends that fact twice
-- below: it is what makes the referral chain provably finite (the
-- foundingReferrer recurrence) and what proves the two promotion rules write
-- two different people (the tier rules).
expose transient shape Join {
    name: text
    referrer: one Member?
}

shape Member {
    name: text
    referrer: one Member?
    tier: text initially "basic"

    -- Who brought this whole branch in: walk the referral chain to its end.
    -- Self-reference one hop through a relationship (README §7) — legal
    -- because the walk provably bottoms out, and the proof input is the
    -- `never` below: no member's referrer can point back at them, so the
    -- chain descends and the base case (`referrer is none`) is reached.
    -- velle: a definition-graph cycle (foundingReferrer -> referrer ->
    -- foundingReferrer) certified by a spendable `never` — checks.md V14,
    -- the acyclicity certificate
    foundingReferrer: one Member? =
        if referrer is none then none
        else if referrer.foundingReferrer is none then referrer
        else referrer.foundingReferrer

    -- The member's current on-time streak: a selector read off the dues
    -- history (the recurrence below), never a stored counter.
    streak: integer =
        if exists DuesPayment for this then latest(DuesPayment for this by paidOn).streakAfter
        else 0
}

rule AdmitMember when Join {
    Member from { name: name, referrer: referrer }
}

never (Member where referrer == this)

-- ── Monthly dues and the on-time streak ──────────────────────────────────────

-- Dues arrive month by month. Whether a payment was on time is read off its
-- own dates, and the streak — consecutive on-time payments — is DERIVED
-- HISTORY, not a stored counter: each payment knows the streak as of itself,
-- by asking its predecessor. The order comes from `paidOn`, a business datum,
-- so a payment that arrives late (posted today, dated last month) slots into
-- its true position and every later payment's streak re-derives to the truth.
-- A stored streak counter updated per arrival would have folded the late
-- payment last and kept the wrong answer (README §19, "Order-dependent folds
-- at a tick: the predecessor recurrence").
-- velle: the predecessor recurrence — a derived to-one `previous` whose
-- selector strictly descends a datum fixed at creation (checks.md V14, the
-- strict-descent certificate); `streakAfter` as readable history; the current
-- value as a selector read
expose shape DuesPayment {
    member: one Member
    coveredMonth: text
    dueBy: Date
    paidOn: Date
    onTime: boolean = if paidOn <= dueBy then true else false
    previous: one DuesPayment? =
        latest(DuesPayment where member == this.member and paidOn < this.paidOn by paidOn)
    streakAfter: integer =
        if not onTime then 0
        else if previous is some then previous.streakAfter + 1
        else 1
}

-- ── Gear purchases and tier promotions ───────────────────────────────────────

-- A big purchase promotes the buyer to gold, and thanks the referrer with
-- advocate status. Both rules fire from the same purchase and both write
-- `tier` — legal only because they provably write two DIFFERENT members:
-- the routes differ by the `referrer` hop, and the `never` above says the
-- hop can't point home. The compiler spends the invariant; without it, this
-- pair is the one-writer error.
-- velle: the instance-aliasing discharge — two writers of one field whose
-- target routes differ by a to-one self-hop, discharged by a spent `never`
-- (checks.md V1/V15; OQ16's PromoteBuyer/PromoteReferrer case)
expose shape Purchase {
    member: one Member
    amount: decimal
}

never (Purchase where amount <= 0)

shape QualifiedPurchase = Purchase where amount >= 100

rule PromoteBuyer when QualifiedPurchase {
    member.tier = "gold"
}

rule PromoteReferrer when (QualifiedPurchase where member.referrer is some) {
    member.referrer.tier = "advocate"
}

-- A gold member's purchases earn a thank-you note, each purchase thanked
-- once. ThankVip's CONDITION reads the tier PromoteBuyer writes, and the two
-- are siblings of one purchase commit — safe, because a commit's firing set
-- is pinned before any sibling runs: the purchase that makes its buyer gold
-- is not yet a gold member's purchase at its own commit; the promotion's
-- commit is where membership flips, and rules react there, in order, as
-- causality. Only a BODY read of a sibling's write would race (checks.md
-- V15), and this body reads nothing the siblings touch.
-- velle: a condition-only read of a sibling's write — accepted; the
-- handled-once anchor (`not exists ThankYouNote for this`) keeps old
-- purchases from re-firing when the member's tier flips later
shape VipPurchase = Purchase where member.tier == "gold"

shape ThankYouNote {
    purchase: one Purchase
}

rule ThankVip when (VipPurchase where not exists ThankYouNote for this) {
    ThankYouNote from { purchase: this }
}

-- ── Certification: waiver plus orientation, then a keycard ───────────────────

-- Enrolling files the safety waiver and books the orientation in one act —
-- two sibling rules, each producing one prerequisite of certification. The
-- siblings are safe in ANY order because both effects push the same way:
-- each is a prerequisite of the same conjunction, so certification lands at
-- whichever effect completes it, in both orders — two prerequisites never
-- race each other (checks.md V15, transition interference: opposite-direction
-- effects are the hazard, same-direction effects are not).
-- velle: same-direction sibling creations under a watched conjunctive
-- refinement — accepted by the polarity analysis
expose shape Enrollment {
    member: one Member
}

shape SafetyWaiver {
    member: one Member
}

shape OrientationRecord {
    member: one Member
}

rule FileWaiver when Enrollment {
    SafetyWaiver from { member: member }
}

rule BookOrientation when Enrollment {
    OrientationRecord from { member: member }
}

shape CertifiedMember = Member where
    exists SafetyWaiver for this and exists OrientationRecord for this

shape Keycard {
    member: one Member
}

rule IssueKeycard when (CertifiedMember where not exists Keycard for this) {
    Keycard from { member: this }
}
